}

Important detail for `connectAiin` in the PR:

```dart
final models = await _fetchLenient(
  () async => (await modelsFetcher(baseUrl, apiKey: [REDACTED:Sensitive Value])).$1,
);
```

The `modelsFetcher` here is `fetchModelsForEndpoint` (default). The baseUrl is `https://api.aiin.by/v1`. Which dialect matches? `_OpenAiCompatibleDialect` (default, last). Since it has no `kind` hint (the AIIN entry is created without a kind), the model list dispatch for later re-picks uses URL matching → OpenAI-compatible → plain GET /models. And AIIN's key is `sk-aiin-…`. That's consistent with the PR's `_landEntry(... modelId: modelId)` without `kind`. OK.

Now — an important observation about the CodeMie re-auth path in `connectCodeMie`:

```dart
final existing = registry.byBaseUrl(baseUrl);
if (existing != null) {
  registry.rememberKey(existing.id, credentials.authToken);
  ...
  return true;
}
```

vs `_landEntry` used in the other flows. Note that `rememberKey` writes the key to the session map AND, when `_useKeychain` is true, persists to Keychain under `keyNameFor(baseUrl)` — host-scoped. But it does NOT persist the key entry-scoped for ChatGPT/AIIN — that's what `_persistEntryScopedKey` does separately. For CodeMie, host-scoped FA_KEY_<HOST> is the canonical slot. Good.

But wait — there's a subtle bug candidate in `connectChatGpt`/`connectAiin`: `_landEntry` may UPDATE an existing entry in place (same name, same baseUrl). For ChatGPT re-auth: `name = _uniqueEntryName(registry, identity, chatGptCodexBaseUrl)`. Consider re-auth for the same account: identity = email, registry.byName(name) returns the existing entry with SAME baseUrl → loop exits immediately → name unchanged → `_landEntry` updates in place (keeps id), then `registry.rememberKey(provider.id, credentials.encode())` + `_persistEntryScopedKey(keyName: keyNameFor(baseUrl, providerName: name))` — same slot name → refresh. Good.

But consider re-auth when the user's email changed case? `byName` folds case. `keyNameFor(baseUrl, providerName: name)` — the canonical algorithm probably normalizes the name too. Minor.

Now — what about a SECOND account with the same email but different case? Edge case, skip.

More interesting: `_uniqueEntryName` uses `registry.byName(name)` which is case-insensitive, and appends `-2`, `-3`. For ChatGPT with two accounts user@x.com and USER@x.com — same byName match (case-insensitive), baseUrl same → treated as re-auth of the first. Display-name collision semantics; fine, matches CLI-ish behavior probably.

Now the CRITICAL issue candidate — in `connectChatGpt`, look at the order of operations:

```dart
final provider = await _landEntry(registry, ...);
